{{also|Reformation|réformation|Réformâtion|re-formation}}
==English==

===Etymology 1===
From {{inh|en|enm|reformacion}}, {{m|enm|reformation}}, from {{der|en|fro|reformacion}} and {{der|en|la|reformatio|refōrmātiō, refōrmātiōnis|reform, change}}, from {{m|la|refōrmō||transform, reshape}}, from {{m|la|re-}} + {{m|la|fōrmō||shape, form}}.

====Pronunciation====
* {{IPA|en|/ˌɹɛfəˈmeɪʃən/|a=UK}}
* {{IPA|en|/ˌɹɛfɚˈmeɪʃən/|a=US}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-reformation.ogg|a=US}}
* {{rhymes|en|eɪʃən|s=4}}

====Noun====
{{en-noun|~}}

# An [[improvement]] (or an intended improvement) in the existing form or [[condition]] of institutions or practices, etc.; intended to make a [[striking]] [[change]] for the better in social, political or religious affairs or in the conduct of persons or operation of organizations.
#* {{RQ:Sidney Arcadia|text={{...}} olde men long nusled in corruption, scorning them that would seeke '''reformation''' {{...}}}}
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Henry 4-1|I|2|text=And like bright metal on a sullen ground,<br>My '''reformation''', glittering o'er my fault,<br>Shall show more goodly, and attract more eyes<br>Than that which hath no foil to set it off.}}
#* {{RQ:Bacon Essayes|chapter=Of Innovation|passage=It is good also, not to try experiments in states, except the necessity be urgent, or the utility evident; and well to beware, that it be the '''reformation''', that draweth on the change, and not the desire of change, that pretendeth the '''reformation'''.}}
#* {{RQ:Dryden Innocence|text={{...}} satire lashes vice into '''reformation''', and humour represents folly so as to render it ridiculous.}}
# {{lb|en|law}} Change or correction, by a court in equity, to a written instrument to conform to the original intention of the parties.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1893|author=Christopher Gustavus Tiedeman|title=A Treatise on Equity Jurisprudence|chapter=Chapter XXIX|section=§507 (footnotes omitted)|text=All sorts of legal instruments may be reformed by equity, when the errors, which have been committed in the execution of them, are mutual mistakes or a mistake of one party combined with the fraud of the other. Thus, '''reformation''' has been decreed of all kinds of deeds of conveyance, including leases, mortgages, deeds of trust, marriage and family settlements. Likewise, bonds of all kinds, policies of insurance, assignments or release of mortgages, executory contracts for the sale of lands, the indorsement of a note, agreements for the establishment of a highway, military orders. So may, also, judgments and other records be corrected or be reformed.}}

